% ============================================================================
% wind-scale — the Beaufort wind FORCE number (0..12) and the descriptive name
% the source gives each force (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the oldest and simplest ideas in weather: instead of a raw wind SPEED,
% describe the wind by what it DOES — smoke that rises straight up, leaves that
% rustle, whole trees in motion, a hurricane that fills the air with foam. The
% Beaufort scale numbers that continuum 0..12, and each number has a fixed
% descriptive NAME: 0 is "Calm", 1 is "Light Air", 6 is "Strong Breeze", 12 is
% "Hurricane". Before you can reason about a marine forecast or a storm warning
% you must first know WHICH force a name is, and WHICH name a force gets. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? beaufort_force_name(6, $Name)     % strong_breeze
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `beaufort_force_name(force, name)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% force number this scale does not define (it never invents a name).
%
% The `force` key is a bare NUMBER (numeric cell, like physics/temperature-
% scales.adj), so a recalled force composes straight into arithmetic and range
% checks. The full scale, as a little truth table:
%
%     force   name              a beginner's cue (what the wind does)
%     -----   ---------------   --------------------------------------------
%       0     calm              smoke rises vertically; sea like a mirror
%       1     light_air         smoke drifts; ripples like scales on the sea
%       2     light_breeze      wind felt on face; leaves rustle
%       3     gentle_breeze     leaves and small twigs in constant motion
%       4     moderate_breeze   raises dust and loose paper; small branches move
%       5     fresh_breeze      small leafy trees begin to sway
%       6     strong_breeze     large branches in motion; whistling heard
%       7     near_gale         whole trees in motion; walking is inconvenient
%       8     gale              breaks twigs off trees; impedes progress
%       9     severe_gale       slight structural damage occurs
%      10     storm             trees uprooted; considerable structural damage
%      11     violent_storm     very rarely experienced; widespread damage
%      12     hurricane         air filled with foam and spray; sea white
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a name and recall its force number:
%
%     ? beaufort_force_name($F, hurricane)   % 12 — reverse recall
%
% A force number this scale does NOT define — 13, -1 — is deliberately NOT a
% row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a name. That honest-
% abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it
% answers exactly the force→name pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% Each `name` value is a SINGLE lowercase atom, the source's own descriptive
% term normalized to one snake_case token ("Light Air" → light_air, "Strong
% Breeze" → strong_breeze) — never a term the source does not print for that
% force, so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every force→name pair below
% was read out of a fetched U.S. National Weather Service page this session, and
% any pair whose name could not be verified there would have been dropped). All
% thirteen names come from the SAME primary U.S. government source, the National
% Weather Service (NWS Miami) "Beaufort Wind Scale" table
% (weather.gov/mfl/beaufort), whose "Description" column prints one descriptive
% term per force. The table row for each force states its number and name
% together; the verbatim terms, force by force:
%
%    0 → calm             "Calm"
%    1 → light_air        "Light Air"
%    2 → light_breeze     "Light Breeze"
%    3 → gentle_breeze    "Gentle Breeze"
%    4 → moderate_breeze  "Moderate Breeze"
%    5 → fresh_breeze     "Fresh Breeze"
%    6 → strong_breeze    "Strong Breeze"
%    7 → near_gale        "Near Gale"
%    8 → gale             "Gale"
%    9 → severe_gale      "Severe Gale"
%   10 → storm            "Storm"
%   11 → violent_storm    "Violent Storm"
%   12 → hurricane        "Hurricane"
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single anchoring span: the NWS term that fixes the
% first row (force 0 → calm). Every OTHER row's name is listed above and comes
% from the same NWS "Description" column, so each remains independently
% checkable. The source is a primary U.S. government National Weather Service
% page (weather.gov), so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table beaufort_force_name {
    columns force, name

    row (0, calm)
    row (1, light_air)
    row (2, light_breeze)
    row (3, gentle_breeze)
    row (4, moderate_breeze)
    row (5, fresh_breeze)
    row (6, strong_breeze)
    row (7, near_gale)
    row (8, gale)
    row (9, severe_gale)
    row (10, storm)
    row (11, violent_storm)
    row (12, hurricane)

    source "Force 0 ... Calm"
    locator "https://www.weather.gov/mfl/beaufort"
    trust authoritative
}
